## Proof: Asymptotic Formula for the Partition Function

**Theorem (Hardy–Ramanujan, 1918).** Let $p(n)$ denote the number of partitions of the positive integer $n$. Then

$$p(n) \sim \frac{1}{4n\sqrt{3}}\,\exp\!\Big(\pi\sqrt{\tfrac{2n}{3}}\Big), \qquad n\to\infty.$$

---

### 1. Generating function and the eta function

The generating function of $p(n)$ is the Euler product

$$F(q):=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}p(n)\,q^{n}=\prod_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{1-q^{m}},\qquad |q|<1.$$

Introduce the Dedekind eta function $\eta(\tau)=e^{\pi i\tau/12}\prod_{m\ge 1}(1-e^{2\pi i m\tau})$, so that with $q=e^{2\pi i\tau}$,

$$F(q)=\frac{q^{1/24}}{\eta(\tau)}.$$

The key input is the **modular transformation**

$$\eta\!\Big(-\frac{1}{\tau}\Big)=\sqrt{-i\tau}\;\eta(\tau),$$

which gives $\eta(\tau)=\eta(-1/\tau)/\sqrt{-i\tau}$.

---

### 2. Asymptotics of $F(e^{-t})$ as $t\to 0^{+}$

Set $q=e^{-t}$, i.e. $\tau=iu$ with $u=t/(2\pi)$. For small $u$, the transformed point $-1/\tau=i/u$ has large imaginary part, so

$$\eta(i/u)=e^{\pi i(i/u)/12}\prod_{m\ge1}(1-e^{-2\pi m/u})\sim e^{-\pi/(12u)}.$$

Using $\eta(iu)=\eta(i/u)/\sqrt{u}$,

$$F(e^{-t})=\frac{e^{-t/24}}{\eta(iu)}\sim e^{-t/24}\,\sqrt{u}\,e^{\pi/(12u)}=\sqrt{\frac{t}{2\pi}}\;\exp\!\Big(\frac{\pi^{2}}{6t}-\frac{t}{24}\Big).$$

The omitted terms come from higher modular images and are exponentially smaller; they affect only lower-order corrections.

---

### 3. Cauchy integral and the saddle point

By Cauchy's formula, for any $t>0$,

$$p(n)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}F(e^{-t+i\theta})\,e^{nt}\,e^{-in\theta}\,d\theta.$$

Write $q=e^{2\pi i\tau}$ with $\tau=x+iu$, $x=\theta/(2\pi)$, $u=t/(2\pi)$. Near $q=1$ (small $x,u$), apply the modular transformation as above:

$$F(q)\approx q^{1/24}\,\sqrt{-i\tau}\,\exp\!\Big(\frac{\pi i x+\pi u}{12(x^{2}+u^{2})}\Big).$$

The full phase of the integrand (as a function of $x$, with $d\theta=2\pi\,dx$) is

$$\Psi(x)=\frac{\pi u}{12(x^{2}+u^{2})}+\frac{\pi i x}{12(x^{2}+u^{2})}+\frac{\pi i x}{12}+2\pi n u-2\pi i n x+\tfrac12\log(-i\tau)-\frac{\pi u}{12}.$$

**Real part (envelope):** $\displaystyle \varphi(x)=\frac{\pi u}{12(x^{2}+u^{2})}+2\pi n u-\frac{\pi u}{12}+\tfrac12\log|{-i\tau}|$, maximized at $x=0$.

**Stationary-phase condition** (vanishing of the derivative of the imaginary part at $x=0$):

$$\frac{\pi}{12u^{2}}-2\pi n=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad u_{n}=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{6n}},\qquad t_{n}=2\pi u_{n}=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{6n}}.$$

---

### 4. Evaluating the exponent at the saddle

At $x=0$, $u=u_{n}$:

$$\frac{\pi}{12u_{n}}+2\pi n u_{n}=\frac{\pi\sqrt{6n}}{6}+\frac{\pi\sqrt{6n}}{6}=\frac{\pi\sqrt{6n}}{3}=\pi\sqrt{\frac{2n}{3}}.$$

(The term $-\pi u_{n}/12=O(n^{-1/2})$ is absorbed into lower-order corrections.) Hence

$$\exp\big(\Psi(0)\big)\sim \sqrt{u_{n}}\;\exp\!\Big(\pi\sqrt{\tfrac{2n}{3}}\Big).$$

---

### 5. The Gaussian integral (prefactor)

The second derivative of the phase at the saddle is

$$\Psi''(0)=-\frac{\pi}{6u^{3}}+O(u^{-2})\sim-\frac{\pi}{6u_{n}^{3}}.$$

The Gaussian integral gives a factor

$$\int e^{\Psi(x)}\,dx\sim e^{\Psi(0)}\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{-\Psi''(0)}}\sim e^{\Psi(0)}\cdot 2\sqrt{3}\,u_{n}^{3/2}.$$

Multiplying by $\sqrt{u_{n}}$ from $e^{\Psi(0)}$:

$$p(n)\sim \exp\!\Big(\pi\sqrt{\tfrac{2n}{3}}\Big)\cdot 2\sqrt{3}\,u_{n}^{2}.$$

Since $u_{n}^{2}=\dfrac{1}{24n}$,

$$2\sqrt{3}\,u_{n}^{2}=\frac{2\sqrt{3}}{24n}=\frac{1}{4n\sqrt{3}}.$$

---

### 6. Conclusion

Combining the exponential and the prefactor,

$$\boxed{\,p(n)\sim \frac{1}{4n\sqrt{3}}\exp\!\Big(\pi\sqrt{\tfrac{2n}{3}}\Big)\,}\qquad(n\to\infty).$$

A more refined analysis (Hardy–Ramanujan–Rademacher) expands this into a convergent series whose first term is exactly the expression above, confirming that the relative error is $O(n^{-1/2})$.

